FMG Client License
Version 1.6, 22 October 2026

Copyright (c) 2026 FMG-PLUGINS. All rights reserved.

This license covers FMG Client: the FMG Client mod for Minecraft, the FMG Launcher (including its
S.T.A.L.K.E.R. 2 tab), the FMG Client installer, the FMG Cosmetics server, and everything that comes
with them (code, cosmetic designs, artwork, backgrounds, texts, the name "FMG Client" and its logo).
Together these are called "the Software" below. "You" means the person using it.

FMG Client is free. You get it without paying anything, and this license tells you what you may
and may not do with it. The FMG Client Terms of Service (TERMS-OF-SERVICE.md, also shown in the
launcher) add the rules for taking part in the online service. You agree to both before you use
the launcher - again each time it is installed or reinstalled, and with every new version of the
FMG Launcher. Sections 8 to 10 below limit the developers' responsibility as far as the law allows;
please read them.


1. WHAT YOU MAY DO

   a) Download the Software from its official sources: the releases page at
      https://github.com/mgnfrdnw-code/FMG-Client/releases and the FMG Launcher's own updater.
   b) Install it and use it on your own computers, for your own personal, non-commercial play.
   c) Share the link to the official releases page with anyone.


2. WHAT YOU MAY NOT DO

   Unless FMG-PLUGINS has given you written permission, you may not:

   a) upload, mirror, re-host or otherwise hand out copies of the Software, whether changed or
      not, or put it into a modpack, launcher or installer of your own. Share the official link
      instead;
   b) sell, rent, lend or charge for the Software, access to it, or anything in it;
   c) modify, decompile, disassemble or reverse engineer the Software, or build other software
      from it, except where the law allows this regardless of what a license says;
   d) remove or change any copyright notice, this license, or the third-party notices;
   e) use the name "FMG Client", its logo or its cosmetic designs for anything else;
   f) attack, overload, automate or trick the FMG Cosmetics server, or use bugs to get coins or
      cosmetics you did not earn. If you find such a bug, please report it instead.


3. COINS, GAMES AND COSMETICS

   a) Coins are a free, just-for-fun points system. They cannot be bought with real money, and
      they cannot be sold, traded, transferred, cashed out or exchanged for money or anything of
      real-world value. The same goes for cosmetics, seasonal ones included.
   b) The games in the launcher (Blackjack, Roulette, Rocket, Penguin, Raining Money, Plinko and the
      seasonal event games) are played with these coins only. No real money is ever wagered, won or
      lost.
   c) Coins and cosmetics are not your property. FMG-PLUGINS may change prices, items, perks, seasons
      and game rules, and may correct or reset balances, for example after a bug or when coins were
      gained by breaking section 2 f). Seasonal cosmetics are only sold during their season; the
      ones you bought stay in your locker. Perks are bought with coins and last the days shown.
      Admins may send coins or cosmetics to one player or to everyone as gifts, and may reset a
      player's coins, cosmetics or a single cosmetic; the player gets a notification. Badges (free
      styles and premium ones bought with coins) are cosmetics too and are seen by other FMG Client
      players.
   d) Pictures you upload for a cape or a shield, shield designs you make and skins you wear must be
      yours or used with permission, and must not be offensive, hateful, sexual or otherwise
      inappropriate. They are visible to other FMG Client players and may be removed at any time.
      Admins may ban an account from FMG Cosmetics for this: what it wears is then hidden from
      everyone, and it cannot buy, change, upload or wear cosmetics until the ban is lifted. The
      player is told, with the reason.


4. ONLINE SERVICES AND YOUR DATA

   a) The Software uses online services: the FMG Cosmetics server (coins, cosmetics, games), the
      releases page (updates), Mojang and Microsoft (sign-in, the game's files, and the capes your
      Minecraft account owns), Fabric's servers (Fabric Loader), Modrinth (add-ons and modpacks you
      browse and install), Open-Meteo (only if you set a town for the weather), e4mc (only while you
      host a world), Discord (only the Discord app on your PC, see g), when you share a clip with a link
      the FMG Cosmetics server's clip storage (or, where that is not set up, a public file host), and on the S.T.A.L.K.E.R. 2 tab Steam and Nexus Mods (see f). Since version 1.3 also:
      Modrinth from inside the game (the Modrinth Packs screen, only when you open it), Mojang's
      public profile service (only when you add a player's skin to your Skin Locker by name), and
      Microsoft Edge WebView2 for Steam's and Nexus Mods' own pages shown inside the launcher. They
      are provided as they are, and may change, pause or stop at any time.
   b) The FMG Cosmetics server stores your Minecraft name and UUID, your coins, your cosmetics,
      your shield designs, your game rounds, pictures you upload, and a record of coin changes. It
      uses this only to run coins, cosmetics and games. What you wear, cape and shield pictures
      included, is shown to other FMG Client players - that is the point of cosmetics. It also notes
      when you last played with FMG Client, so that for 30 days after that the FMG badge next to
      your name tells other FMG Client players, on any server, that you use it too. Since version
      1.3 it also keeps notifications for you (gifts, coins from an admin, a ban), the perks you
      bought, a ban from cosmetics if there is one, and - for three days after you change your skin
      with FMG Client - the textures.minecraft.net address of that skin, so FMG Client players see
      it before every server does. Since version 1.4 it keeps the badge you picked (a free style and
      its colour, or a premium badge you own). Since version 1.6 it keeps clips you share with a link
      for 72 hours (who shared them, their size and when - see the Terms of Service, 6 d), the
      telemetry of h) while it is on, and a security log for every account: sign-ins, purchases, uploads, skin and badge
      changes, perks, admin actions on the account, switching telemetry off, and admins looking at
      the account's telemetry, each with the time, the IP address and the program version, kept 90
      days and sealed so that changes show - to protect accounts and to investigate abuse or a hacked
      account; it is handed to authorities only where the law requires or allows it (Terms of
      Service, section 8). Admins can see names, coins, what an account owns, when it last played,
      whether it is banned from cosmetics, its telemetry and its security log - nothing else.
      Nothing is sold, used for advertising or given to anyone else.
   c) The launcher never sees your Microsoft password: you sign in on Microsoft's own page. Your
      login tokens are only sent to Microsoft and Mojang, never to the FMG Cosmetics server or
      anyone else. Signing in to the cosmetics server sends Mojang's public certificate for your
      account and a signature made with it, which proves which Minecraft account you use. Wearing
      one of your Minecraft capes, and changing your skin (the Skin Locker, in the launcher and in
      game), is done with Mojang directly, like in the official launcher. Your skins are kept in
      the skins folder next to the game, on your PC.
   d) The weather on the Play page's background is only looked up if you type a town in Settings.
      The launcher then sends that name to Open-Meteo (open-meteo.com) once to find the place, and
      afterwards only the place's coordinates, about every 20 minutes while the launcher is open.
      Nothing else about you is sent, and no place is ever worked out by itself. Remove the town,
      or turn the weather off, to stop it. Day and night are worked out on your PC, offline.
   e) Hosting a world for friends (Open to LAN with hosting on) runs the e4mc mod, which the launcher
      downloads from Modrinth. While you host, your world's connection goes through e4mc's relay
      (e4mc.link) so friends anywhere can join with the address it gives you. Nothing is hosted
      when you do not open your world.
   f) The S.T.A.L.K.E.R. 2 tab:
      - Linking your Steam account: you sign in on Steam's own page in your browser. The launcher
        never sees your Steam password; it only receives your public Steam ID, has Steam confirm it,
        and keeps it and your public Steam name on your PC to see whether the copy of the game on
        this PC is yours. Steam is only started by the launcher (quietly, in the tray) once that is
        confirmed - when the launcher starts, and when an update for the game is out - and you can
        switch this off.
      - Patch notes come from Steam's public news for the game, and the Steam Workshop is read from
        Steam's public pages. Subscribing to Workshop items is done by Steam itself.
      - Nexus Mods: browsing needs nothing. Installing needs your own Nexus Mods account: the
        personal API key you paste is kept on your PC, encrypted with Windows for your Windows user,
        and is only ever sent to Nexus Mods' own API. Downloads only take Nexus Mods' own routes
        (direct for Premium members, Nexus' "Mod Manager Download" button for everyone else), never
        a way around them. If you allow it, Nexus' "Mod Manager Download" links open the launcher;
        switching that off gives them back to the program that had them before.
      - Steam's store page and Nexus Mods' pages can open in a small window inside the launcher
        (Microsoft Edge WebView2). They are those services' own pages: what you type into them,
        including a sign-in, goes to them only, and the launcher does not read it. Their sign-ins
        stay in the launcher's own browser folder (launcher\webview; deleting it signs you out).
        From Nexus Mods the launcher only takes what Nexus itself hands over for a mod: its "Mod
        Manager Download" link, or a mod's archive you download there yourself, which is kept in
        launcher\downloads (deleted after a day) and installed.
      - Mods are made by other people, under their own terms, and are not checked by FMG-PLUGINS.
        You install them at your own risk; keep a copy of your saves.
   g) Discord Rich Presence (on by default, off in Settings) shows in your Discord status which
      game and page you are on in the launcher. Since version 1.3 the game shows "FMG Client" there
      too while the launcher is closed (Right Shift > Discord Status turns that off). Both only talk
      to the Discord app running on your PC; nothing is sent while it is off or Discord is closed.
   h) Telemetry ("Share telemetry data", since version 1.6 of this license, FMG Launcher 1.4): on by
      default and switched off at any time on the agreement screen or in Settings > Launcher >
      Privacy. While it is on, the launcher sends the FMG Cosmetics server data about the install
      (versions; Windows version, build and language; processor, memory, graphics card and driver;
      screens; free disk space; the launcher's settings; the Minecraft mods and their versions, the
      number of texture packs and the shader; S.T.A.L.K.E.R. 2's build and branch; counts of feature
      use) and about what happened (launcher and game starts and ends; Minecraft crash reports and,
      after an error, the end of the game's log; launcher crashes and errors; failed or large
      downloads; clip uploads), and what FMG Client wrote down in the game (its mods that are on,
      Java and the graphics driver, video settings, the servers you joined with time, duration, ping
      and why you left - home network, plain IP and e4mc addresses only as such -, frame-rate
      summaries and errors of its mods - the game itself never sends anything). Windows
      user and PC names, profile folders and anything that looks like a token are removed first.
      Chats, keystrokes, the content of your files, passwords, tokens, account details and browsing
      are never collected. While the launcher is signed in, telemetry is linked to your Minecraft
      account, so a problem you report can be looked into. Events are kept 90 days, an install's
      details until 120 days after its last report; switching telemetry off stops it at once and the
      server deletes that install's (and, signed in, your account's) telemetry 14 days later. The
      legal basis is our legitimate interest in finding and fixing problems and in security (Art. 6
      (1) (f) GDPR) - you can object by switching it off - and, where it is needed, your consent
      under Section 25 (1) TDDDG, given by leaving the switch on and withdrawn by switching it off.
      Details, and your rights: Terms of Service, section 7.
   i) Streamer Mode (Settings > Launcher) hides the town for the weather, account details and IDs in
      the launcher while you share your screen. It changes nothing that is sent anywhere.
   j) FMG Clips' "Fix Microphone Access" button (since version 1.6, FMG Client 1.5), and only when
      you press it: sets Windows' microphone permission for desktop programs to "allowed" for your
      own Windows account, so clips can record your microphone. It changes nothing else, never for
      other accounts, and Windows' Settings (Privacy > Microphone) can switch it back at any time.
   k) Access may be removed for anyone who breaks this license.


